inspired by the recent "dreamtime" YANI: Meditation.

Some named monsters meditate, so why shouldn't the player?

For the command to be successful, the player must not be confused or
stunned, and first #sit without getting an "uncomfortable" message.

Not sure how polymorphed players should be handled. Some monsters may
be suited to meditation, but others much less so. You also raise
issues about sitting and comfort (ie egg layers and sitting, and are
there any monsters that can't sit?) so it may be easier to just
disallow polymorphed meditation altogether.

Anyway, once the prerequisites are met, the player can issue the
#meditate command.
Meditating players remain motionless as if asleep, and, like sleeping
characters, may take several turns to be roused by approaching or
attacking monsters. If allowed to meditate for long enough (is a
hundred or so turns too much?), the character gets a beneficial
effect, detailled later. Meditation would be a skill that can be
practised (quite quickly, only a handful of successful attempts would
be required to advance a level.)

Players without sleep resistance have a chance of drifting off to
sleep while attempting to meditate, especially if satiated and/or in
an unlit area. Although you would expect meditation to involve sitting
perfectly still, dust Elbereths would soon be degraded by a meditating
character shuffling around to get comfortable.

Meditating players can be distracted, obviously: Not just by combat
but by teleportitis/ polymorphitis (meditation is great for IDing
rings!), by hunger and even by loud noises (explosions, doors opening,
maybe even shopkeepers or vault guards). Once distracted, the player
has either give up the meditation attempt to start all over again.
Minor distractions can be almost entirely mitigated by placing a lit
candle on an adjacent square. (This means that while meditating in a
locked single-square cupboard is safer, it's less likely to be
effective.) Of course candles tend to be fairly rare until minetown,
so maybe some classes (monk, priest, arc) could start with one or two
in inventory, or something else could be done to increase their early-
game frequency a little. Altars could have a chance of being generated
with a few candles on them, for example, or certain early-game
monsters (dwarves? hobbits? gnomes?)might be given a small chance of
carrying them in inventory.

At unskilled, the end result of a successful meditation would be a
partial enlightenment effect, showing a random selection of
information from their own resistances, attributes and prayer status.
partial enlightenment effect, showing a random selection of
information from their own resistances, attributes and prayer status.
At basic, meditation would give quicker and better results than
unskilled; less chance of dozing off, less shuffling, immunity to
minor distractions, a quicker 'snap out of it' response when under
attack and a possibility of a unihorn-like 'stat restore' effect in
addition to the unskilled enlightenment effect.
At skilled, the player benefits from further enhancements to the ease
and efectiveness as from unskilled>basic, but also gets quicker
health/ mana regeneration while meditating as well as full
enlightenment and stat-restore. Other effects might be worth adding,
maybe something like slashem's "reinforce memory" technique, which
prolongs spell-memory.
I don't think there needs to be a level above skilled.

Only knights, rangers, samurai, wizards, healers, monks and priests
can get to basic. Only monks and maybe priests and samurai would be
able to get to skilled. I'd like to put wizards up there too but they
get enough perks as it is. That list of classes may need to be argued
further, I'm not sure.

You'll notice I didn't mention hallucination as a barrier to
mediatation earlier. It could be argued that halluciantion should give
easier access to higher states of consciousness, but an equally strong
argument could be made against it. Therefore all hallucinatiing
meditation, regardless of other factors, should result in a 20% chance
of extremely effective meditation (only takes a handful of turns, full
enlightenment, unihorm-type stat restoration, mana and HP boost,
hallucination cured afterwards) or an 80% chance of several hundred
turns of completely pointless and undistracted (ie, even combat might
not be enough to bring them out of it) bellybutton de-fluffing.

I think this would add another worthwhile choice of skill-slots. The
effects would be very handy for early-game characters who want to ID
rings, restore their stats of ascertain their prayer status. However
it comes with drawbacks: Time spent sat around meditating is time
burning nutrition, and there is a risk of getting eaten before you can
come out of your meditative state (although that can be mitigated, to
a certain extent: Elbereth, locked doors, pets.) Practise should be
quick enough that a player can reasonably enhance to basic before they
see their first unihorn, making the effects worthwhile before they
become largely obselete, but of course at the price of spent skill
slots.



